  "answer": "The Python PEP 20, \"The Zen of Python,\" states that \"Namespaces are one honking great idea -- let's do more of those!\" [1].\n\nFurthermore, namespaces are defined as a mapping from names to objects [4]. Examples of namespaces include:\n*   The set of built-in names (containing functions such as `abs()`, and built-in exception names).\n*   The global names in a module.\n*   The local names in a function invocation.\n*   The set of attributes of an object also forms a namespace [4].\n\nOne module has its own private namespace, which is used as the global namespace for all functions defined within that module [5]. If you know what you are doing, you can access module's global variables using the notation `modname.itemname` [5].\n\nImporting everything in a Python package into a particular variable name",
